1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:29,980
Let's pray.

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Let's pray.

3
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:59,980
Well, good morning, Canvas Community. I think it's the first time in a long time Danny walked me through everything to do with the mic, and I didn't mess it up, so thanks, Danny.

4
00:00:59,980 --> 00:01:08,980
Well, good morning. My name is Corey Smith, and I have the privilege of being part of the teaching team here at Canvas Community.

5
00:01:08,980 --> 00:01:21,980
Pastor Ryan talked a little bit about the sports ball stuff. If you want hospitality with sports ball, you talk to him. If you just want to not talk about sports ball, and we can do something, talk to me.

6
00:01:21,980 --> 00:01:29,980
So, we've got you covered. We've got the bases covered. If we haven't had the opportunity to meet, I'd like to throw out, introduce yourself.

7
00:01:29,980 --> 00:01:35,980
I find it more and more awkward that people know who I am, and I don't know your name, and I pride myself on trying to remember a lot of names.

8
00:01:35,980 --> 00:01:46,980
So, find me. We'll do a practice, see if I can remember your name. So, last week, Pastor Ryan began a series called Witness through our latest installment of Practice in the Way.

9
00:01:46,980 --> 00:01:55,980
Throughout these four weeks, we are learning that followers of Jesus demonstrate and declare the reign of Jesus wherever we live, work, and play.

10
00:01:55,980 --> 00:02:06,980
What does that look like? I'm glad you asked. We demonstrate the reign of Jesus through the way we live, and we declare his reign through the words we speak.

11
00:02:06,980 --> 00:02:14,980
And I think Christian Witness should hold these two things together. I think we've gotten really good at declaring and not really good at living.

12
00:02:14,980 --> 00:02:25,980
So, what I want you to do is spend some time today during this message asking God, "What area in my life do I maybe lean one way instead of the other?

13
00:02:25,980 --> 00:02:35,980
And Lord, how do I hold these two things in tension?" Because I think if we only speak, our lives contradict our message.

14
00:02:35,980 --> 00:02:43,980
People have every reason to question what we say. But if we attempt to live good lives while never naming Jesus,

15
00:02:43,980 --> 00:02:48,980
people may only see our kindness without ever meeting our King.

16
00:02:48,980 --> 00:02:55,980
So our refrain throughout the series is, "The reign of Jesus is demonstrated through our lives and declared by our words."

17
00:02:55,980 --> 00:03:05,980
Last week, Pastor Ryan began with love, and witness does not begin with guilt, pressure, or an obligation to turn a person into a project.

18
00:03:05,980 --> 00:03:11,980
It begins where the love of Christ begins changing what we see when we look at people.

19
00:03:11,980 --> 00:03:17,980
Christ's love should move us towards people.

20
00:03:17,980 --> 00:03:24,980
But love needs somewhere to go. Compassion must eventually take up space in our actual lives.

21
00:03:24,980 --> 00:03:33,980
Love becomes visible when it makes room for someone else.

22
00:03:33,980 --> 00:03:43,980
And that brings us to the practice of hospitality. And the idea, I would say, is radical hospitality.

23
00:03:43,980 --> 00:03:51,980
Hospitality is defined as the ordinary practice of making people feel wanted, welcomed, or given room in our lives.

24
00:03:51,980 --> 00:03:53,980
And that's how I'm going to define it for this message.

25
00:03:53,980 --> 00:03:58,980
Because I think when some of us hear the word hospitality, we immediately picture entertaining.

26
00:03:58,980 --> 00:04:07,980
We imagine a spotless house, matching dishes, an expensive meal, seasonal decorations that we dug out of the basement.

27
00:04:07,980 --> 00:04:13,980
A host that somehow has every room smelling like cinnamon or lavender or...

28
00:04:13,980 --> 00:04:19,980
And we just finished cleaning before you guys arrived because we keep a spotless house.

29
00:04:19,980 --> 00:04:27,980
If that is hospitality, many of us have already disqualified ourselves.

30
00:04:27,980 --> 00:04:32,980
Because we're real people, real lives, learn to follow Jesus.

31
00:04:32,980 --> 00:04:39,980
The excuses are, right, our house is too small. Our children are too loud. Our schedule is too full.

32
00:04:39,980 --> 00:04:45,980
We are not that good at cooks. The living room is not clean. I don't have the personality for it.

33
00:04:45,980 --> 00:04:50,980
Maybe that's just me. You guys are probably more spiritual than that.

34
00:04:50,980 --> 00:04:53,980
So this is probably going to be an awkward sermon if it's just me and not you guys.

35
00:04:53,980 --> 00:04:58,980
But the idea is that biblical hospitality is not entertainment.

36
00:04:58,980 --> 00:05:06,980
It is the ordinary practice of making another person feel wanted, welcome, or given room in our life.

37
00:05:06,980 --> 00:05:12,980
And that, folks, may be more difficult than preparing an impressive meal.

38
00:05:12,980 --> 00:05:17,980
See, I think modern homes, and I spent some time this week looking at my own house,

39
00:05:17,980 --> 00:05:22,980
modern homes are wonderfully designed for privacy.

40
00:05:22,980 --> 00:05:31,980
We pull into our garage, the door closes behind us, and we enter the house without ever encountering anyone.

41
00:05:31,980 --> 00:05:36,980
I live in the woods. That's probably a little bit harder to do in a subdivision, but I still think you guys can pull it off.

42
00:05:36,980 --> 00:05:42,980
The garage door, I think, begins to function, if we're being honest, like a drawbridge on a castle.

43
00:05:42,980 --> 00:05:51,980
So we return from work, close the gate, eat, stream something, put the kids down, sleep,

44
00:05:51,980 --> 00:05:58,980
repeat the same process tomorrow. And don't hear that as our homes should not be a place of rest.

45
00:05:58,980 --> 00:06:04,980
Don't get me wrong there. There's nothing wrong with needing privacy, quiet, or time together as a family.

46
00:06:04,980 --> 00:06:12,980
But what I encourage you to do is do not let your place of rest become the bunker where you can hide from the world.

47
00:06:12,980 --> 00:06:20,980
The gifts God has given us for life can become walls that we use to insulate ourselves from the lives of others.

48
00:06:20,980 --> 00:06:33,980
And I think if we're honest with ourselves, we hear some criticism shot towards Christians that strike a nerve.

49
00:06:33,980 --> 00:06:46,980
I think people, I've heard people say, if you would look at the life of Jesus, if Christians actually believe Jesus, why don't they do the things Jesus did and told them to do?

50
00:06:46,980 --> 00:06:56,980
Why does it so many Christians seem protected or insulated from the poor, the lonely, the hurting, the outsider, and the people Jesus continually moved towards?

51
00:06:56,980 --> 00:07:06,980
And that criticism is sometimes unfair. I think it can overlook countless acts of quiet faithfulness that never become visible online or in the mouths of our critics.

52
00:07:06,980 --> 00:07:15,980
Only God knows our faithful acts in the quiet. But I think if we're honest, and we should be humble enough to hear it, there's truth in it, too.

53
00:07:15,980 --> 00:07:22,980
Jesus did not merely form the church so that we could preserve the institution itself.

54
00:07:22,980 --> 00:07:30,980
Now, I believe that Christ formed a people who would embody and announce his kingdom.

55
00:07:30,980 --> 00:07:44,980
So the church, I think, is meant to be a healed and healing community, a people through whom the world can experience a foretaste of the welcome, reconciliation, and restoration of God.

56
00:07:44,980 --> 00:07:52,980
We're gathered around the table of Jesus so that we also can carry the welcome of Jesus out into the world.

57
00:07:52,980 --> 00:08:01,980
So the question underneath today's message is, and if you're a note taker, I want you to write this down, who has access to your life?

58
00:08:01,980 --> 00:08:13,980
Not simply who would be allowed in your house if they ask, but for whom have you intentionally made room at your table, in your schedule, and in your relationships?

59
00:08:13,980 --> 00:08:18,980
Let's turn together to Luke 19. I'll give you a couple minutes to get there.

60
00:08:18,980 --> 00:08:26,980
We're going to look at Luke 19:1-10. I think we have the CSB version on the screen. I'll be reading that with you as well.

61
00:08:26,980 --> 00:08:37,980
Jesus entered Jericho and was passing through. There was a man named Zacchaeus who was a chief tax collector, and he was rich.

62
00:08:37,980 --> 00:08:44,980
He was trying to see who Jesus was, but he was not able because of the crowd, since he was a short man.

63
00:08:44,980 --> 00:08:50,980
So running ahead, he climbed up into a sycamore tree to see Jesus, since he was about to pass that way.

64
00:08:50,980 --> 00:08:57,980
When Jesus came to the place, he looked up and said to him, "Zacchaeus, hurry and come down,

65
00:08:57,980 --> 00:09:02,980
because today it is necessary for me to stay at your house."

66
00:09:02,980 --> 00:09:08,980
So he quickly came down and welcomed him joyfully. All who saw it began to complain,

67
00:09:08,980 --> 00:09:12,980
"He's gone to stay with a sinful man."

68
00:09:12,980 --> 00:09:19,980
But Zacchaeus stood there and said to the Lord, "Look, I'll give half my possessions to the poor.

69
00:09:19,980 --> 00:09:27,980
Lord, and if I've extorted anything from anyone, I'll pay back four times as much."

70
00:09:27,980 --> 00:09:34,980
Jesus says, and I love this, "Today salvation has come to this house," Jesus told him.

71
00:09:34,980 --> 00:09:44,980
"Because he too is a son of Abraham, for the Son of Man has come to seek and to save the lost."

72
00:09:44,980 --> 00:09:50,980
This is the word of the Lord. I think if you grew up in Sunday school like I did,

73
00:09:50,980 --> 00:09:57,980
I found myself in a place where it was difficult to take the story of Zacchaeus seriously.

74
00:09:57,980 --> 00:10:07,980
You know where I'm going. Zacchaeus was a wee little man. Good church people in the crowd. I like it.

75
00:10:07,980 --> 00:10:13,980
And a wee little man was he. I won't do that. Maybe Liz can come back up after we're done.

76
00:10:13,980 --> 00:10:21,980
She's pumped today. She can probably lead us in that. But seriously, I think I picture, I won't put that on you all,

77
00:10:21,980 --> 00:10:32,980
but I picture a cheerful little fellow who probably looks similar to a Keebler elf climbing a tree because he couldn't see over the tall people.

78
00:10:32,980 --> 00:10:41,980
But to the people of Jericho, Zacchaeus was not cute. He might have been short, but he's definitely not cute.

79
00:10:41,980 --> 00:10:48,980
And to his neighbors, he's despised. And Luke tells us that he was not merely just a tax collector.

80
00:10:48,980 --> 00:10:53,980
He was the chief tax collector, and he was pretty rich.

81
00:10:53,980 --> 00:11:01,980
And if we don't know, tax collectors worked for the Roman Empire, the occupying power controlling the Jewish nation at this time.

82
00:11:01,980 --> 00:11:06,980
So Rome would determine the amount of taxes you would owe Rome.

83
00:11:06,980 --> 00:11:13,980
But the collectors would be like, "Hey, add a couple percentage points to that. I'll add to my coffers," and we're all happy.

84
00:11:13,980 --> 00:11:21,980
So tax collectors would get rich by charging more, and the power of the empire would stand behind that demand the tax collectors would make.

85
00:11:21,980 --> 00:11:25,980
You didn't have a choice. You didn't get to negotiate. I love negotiating.

86
00:11:25,980 --> 00:11:31,980
But Rome determined the amount of required, and collectors would enrich themselves by more.

87
00:11:31,980 --> 00:11:41,980
So Zacchaeus had probably become extremely wealthy by participating in the machine that impoverished his neighbor and his kinfolk.

88
00:11:41,980 --> 00:11:45,980
That's an Appalachian word if you guys don't know it. And it slipped right out.

89
00:11:45,980 --> 00:11:51,980
So he probably got rich by impoverishing his neighbors.

90
00:11:51,980 --> 00:11:56,980
He had not accidentally wandered into the margins. He didn't probably inherit this wealth.

91
00:11:56,980 --> 00:12:02,980
But he profited by helping place other people in a spot where they were impoverished.

92
00:12:02,980 --> 00:12:09,980
He was ethnically a son of Abraham, but his neighbors would see him as a collaborator of the empire.

93
00:12:09,980 --> 00:12:16,980
He lived among them, but he was betraying them. I don't know how often they collected taxes in Rome,

94
00:12:16,980 --> 00:12:23,980
but he was betraying them on a daily basis. He had money and power and no place of honor among his people.

95
00:12:23,980 --> 00:12:30,980
But, and this is the beauty of the story, then Jesus comes through Jericho.

96
00:12:30,980 --> 00:12:37,980
Zacchaeus wanted to see him, and we don't get the backstory. We don't know why. Maybe it was rumors.

97
00:12:37,980 --> 00:12:46,980
Maybe he's curious. Maybe he'd heard stories. Maybe he had begun experiencing something in him he couldn't explain.

98
00:12:46,980 --> 00:12:57,980
So he runs ahead and climbs the tree. So the beauty in Zacchaeus, in that Zacchaeus is willing to look ridiculous just to catch a glimpse of Jesus.

99
00:12:57,980 --> 00:13:05,980
And I would ask if we would do the same. Zacchaeus climbs a tree because he wants to see Jesus.

100
00:13:05,980 --> 00:13:10,980
So when Jesus reaches that place, Jesus looks up and sees him before Zacchaeus can say anything.

101
00:13:10,980 --> 00:13:18,980
Jesus calls to him by name. Zacchaeus, hurry and come down because it is necessary for me to stay at your house.

102
00:13:18,980 --> 00:13:22,980
For him going to your house? Yeah.

103
00:13:22,980 --> 00:13:32,980
Jesus does not say, and you can put this on me, I would like to schedule coffee so we can discuss some of your financial practices.

104
00:13:32,980 --> 00:13:38,980
He does not tell Zacchaeus, "Hey man, we've got some problems. We've got to clean them up.

105
00:13:38,980 --> 00:13:44,980
We've got to make some restitution. Submit an application. Maybe we can have dinner sometime."

106
00:13:44,980 --> 00:13:51,980
Now Jesus, in the Jesus way, says, "I am coming to your house."

107
00:13:51,980 --> 00:13:59,980
And Luke, and I love this idea, and I put a little bit of emphasis on it as I was reading, Luke uses the language of necessity.

108
00:13:59,980 --> 00:14:05,980
It is necessary that Jesus stay there.

109
00:14:05,980 --> 00:14:12,980
So I think we get this idea that Jesus is like sidetracked by his ministry.

110
00:14:12,980 --> 00:14:18,980
And I would very much encourage you to think of this. This meal is not an interruption to mission.

111
00:14:18,980 --> 00:14:22,980
This is Jesus's mission. This is the whole mission of Jesus.

112
00:14:22,980 --> 00:14:28,980
This is God in the flesh seeking his estranged creation.

113
00:14:28,980 --> 00:14:33,980
I'm going to use a cool seminary word and write this down. If you don't know it, this is the Missio Dei.

114
00:14:33,980 --> 00:14:42,980
This is the mission of God. And Zacchaeus comes down and welcomes him joyfully.

115
00:14:42,980 --> 00:14:49,980
And everybody said, "Amen. Hallelujah." Because they were probably all good people.

116
00:14:49,980 --> 00:14:54,980
Wrong. No, right. We read it. Everyone complains about it. Not a few people.

117
00:14:54,980 --> 00:15:00,980
Luke says, "All who saw began to complain." And I'm not sure that this is a hyperbole.

118
00:15:00,980 --> 00:15:06,980
Not sure that Luke is expanding there. I think everybody in the crowd was probably like, "That guy's a jerk."

119
00:15:06,980 --> 00:15:09,980
He's gone to stay with a sinful man.

120
00:15:09,980 --> 00:15:14,980
See, I think the people were offended not merely because Jesus spoke to Zacchaeus.

121
00:15:14,980 --> 00:15:18,980
I'm getting all my prophets mixed up. Because Jesus spoke to Zacchaeus.

122
00:15:18,980 --> 00:15:24,980
They're offended because Jesus would even consider to entertain company of this sinful man.

123
00:15:24,980 --> 00:15:31,980
And this is actually a really cool part that I had to wrestle with this week, that meals communicate belonging.

124
00:15:31,980 --> 00:15:37,980
To share a table was to share life. It was an offer of peace, relationship, and welcome.

125
00:15:37,980 --> 00:15:41,980
And we even get some of that today, but tables brought people together.

126
00:15:41,980 --> 00:15:47,980
But tables also established boundaries. They show who belonged to whom.

127
00:15:47,980 --> 00:15:52,980
And even in our Western 21st century culture, I think our tables still do that.

128
00:15:52,980 --> 00:15:57,980
It's not been so long ago that I was in a high school.

129
00:15:57,980 --> 00:16:02,980
But I want you to picture a high school cafeteria.

130
00:16:02,980 --> 00:16:04,980
Give you a second.

131
00:16:04,980 --> 00:16:08,980
Nobody has to put a signpost like explaining the different groups, right?

132
00:16:08,980 --> 00:16:13,980
The social map of who's setting where. You can usually point it out.

133
00:16:13,980 --> 00:16:20,980
And for those of you in the congregation that may have gotten married recently, I want you to think about a wedding seating chart

134
00:16:20,980 --> 00:16:24,980
and the stress behind that.

135
00:16:24,980 --> 00:16:30,980
Deciding who sits together quickly reveals relationships, history, tension, boundaries.

136
00:16:30,980 --> 00:16:35,980
We're not putting those two together because I don't want to fight.

137
00:16:35,980 --> 00:16:43,980
As a general rule, if we're being honest, I think the general rule is we tend to want to eat with our friends and family.

138
00:16:43,980 --> 00:16:49,980
We sit with people we already know, people who make us feel comfortable, people who are likely to invite us over,

139
00:16:49,980 --> 00:16:56,980
and return the invitation, and people who probably won't complicate our evening any more than we want it to be.

140
00:16:56,980 --> 00:17:01,980
But I think Jesus ate with all the wrong people.

141
00:17:01,980 --> 00:17:07,980
See, Luke's gospel is saturated. If you would take the time to read it with this point in mind,

142
00:17:07,980 --> 00:17:11,980
that Jesus eats with tax collectors and sinners. He eats at the home of Pharisees.

143
00:17:11,980 --> 00:17:17,980
He feeds hungry crowds. He tells hosts to invite people who cannot repay him.

144
00:17:17,980 --> 00:17:20,980
He breaks bread with his disciples on the night he is betrayed.

145
00:17:20,980 --> 00:17:26,980
And after the resurrection, he becomes known to the visitors of Emmaus, breaking bread together.

146
00:17:26,980 --> 00:17:30,980
See, one scholar famously summarized Luke's gospel as saying Jesus is either, in Luke's gospel,

147
00:17:30,980 --> 00:17:35,980
Jesus is either going to a meal, at a meal, or coming from a meal.

148
00:17:35,980 --> 00:17:39,980
See, Jesus tells us his mission when we read Luke 19:10,

149
00:17:39,980 --> 00:17:44,980
"The Son of Man has come to seek and to save the lost."

150
00:17:44,980 --> 00:17:50,980
And if we read back earlier in Luke 7:34, his critics describe one of his methods as,

151
00:17:50,980 --> 00:17:56,980
"The Son of Man has come eating and drinking."

152
00:17:56,980 --> 00:18:01,980
He came to seek and to save. He came eating and drinking.

153
00:18:01,980 --> 00:18:05,980
See, I think there's a beauty in this, that Jesus announces the kingdom of God,

154
00:18:05,980 --> 00:18:12,980
but he is also allowing people a foretaste of what the kingdom of God is like.

155
00:18:12,980 --> 00:18:18,980
Around his tables, people who have been treated as categories became people again.

156
00:18:18,980 --> 00:18:25,980
I love this line. It's not in my notes, but it was one of the most impactful lines from my missiology class,

157
00:18:25,980 --> 00:18:31,980
is that God taking on flesh gave humanity its dignity back.

158
00:18:31,980 --> 00:18:35,980
And I think Jesus did that with tables and meals.

159
00:18:35,980 --> 00:18:40,980
He gave, he began restoring dignity to his lost creation, the people who he loved,

160
00:18:40,980 --> 00:18:44,980
who bear his image.

161
00:18:44,980 --> 00:18:49,980
The unwelcome received welcome, the overlooked were seen, the unclean were touched,

162
00:18:49,980 --> 00:18:53,980
the ashamed heard their names spoken without disgust on their lips.

163
00:18:53,980 --> 00:19:01,980
At the table of Jesus, grace is not merely explained, it is embodied.

164
00:19:01,980 --> 00:19:06,980
This is what makes hospitality an act of witness.

165
00:19:06,980 --> 00:19:09,980
And I don't want you to hear something I'm not saying, right?

166
00:19:09,980 --> 00:19:13,980
Hospitality is not the whole gospel, right?

167
00:19:13,980 --> 00:19:21,980
But I think it creates a social reality in which the gospel can be felt tangibly.

168
00:19:21,980 --> 00:19:32,980
Before many people can hear the welcome of the king, they may need to experience that welcome through the king's people.

169
00:19:32,980 --> 00:19:38,980
I just want to sit there a minute. I can tell you a lot of things.

170
00:19:38,980 --> 00:19:44,980
But unless you experience from me, are you more apt to believe the things I'm telling you?

171
00:19:44,980 --> 00:19:51,980
I mean, let's be honest. Not that I, by any stretch of the imagination, am perfect.

172
00:19:51,980 --> 00:19:57,980
I constantly make mistakes that I have to repent or ask forgiveness for.

173
00:19:57,980 --> 00:20:07,980
But that too, I think, is a witness to the king who I proclaim to serve, that I'm willing to try to right my wrongs in his name because I think he has called me to something.

174
00:20:07,980 --> 00:20:14,980
There's a beautiful reversal in this story.

175
00:20:14,980 --> 00:20:23,980
See, I think the beauty is Jesus doesn't own the table, and this is going to be completely weird to us in our context, but I want us to sit with it.

176
00:20:23,980 --> 00:20:30,980
He does not invite Zacchaeus to his own home. Jesus is essentially homeless at this point.

177
00:20:30,980 --> 00:20:36,980
Jesus practices hospitality by asking Zacchaeus to receive him.

178
00:20:36,980 --> 00:20:45,980
We're tracking. And I think a lot of my church people are like, "Isn't that the opposite of hospitality to invite yourself over for dinner?"

179
00:20:45,980 --> 00:20:54,980
And I think it's really cool because I think some of us assume we cannot practice hospitality unless we have something to offer.

180
00:20:54,980 --> 00:21:05,980
But Jesus enters another person's space. He allows Zacchaeus to contribute, and he receives what Zacchaeus can give.

181
00:21:05,980 --> 00:21:12,980
I've told this story before. My pastor growing up would always tell me if he would do something nice for me.

182
00:21:12,980 --> 00:21:18,980
I'm like, "I can't accept that." He said, you know, "Eric, what's he say?" "Don't steal my blessing."

183
00:21:18,980 --> 00:21:30,980
And there's truth in that. Hospitality can happen in a living room, but it can also happen in a restaurant, a break room, a park, a dorm, a truck, or even across the backyard fence.

184
00:21:30,980 --> 00:21:34,980
Big fan of home improvement growing up.

185
00:21:34,980 --> 00:21:38,980
It can look like sharing a meal. It can look like accepting someone else's invitation.

186
00:21:38,980 --> 00:21:47,980
It can look like remembering a name, asking a real question, or maybe even just putting our phones away and giving somebody our full attention.

187
00:21:47,980 --> 00:21:56,980
Listening can also be hospitality, and I think it's something missing in our world.

188
00:21:56,980 --> 00:22:03,980
The idea of receiving another person's story without immediately correcting, categorizing, or managing them

189
00:22:03,980 --> 00:22:12,980
creates room for them to be heard. Accepting another person's gift can be hospitality because it allows that person

190
00:22:12,980 --> 00:22:18,980
to become more than the recipient of our generosity. I'm going to probably go over our time,

191
00:22:18,980 --> 00:22:23,980
but I'm telling a little side stories here. Matt Lewis one time, if he's listening,

192
00:22:23,980 --> 00:22:32,980
I hope he hears his wisdom come through and doesn't hear me butchering it. But the idea was, Corey, do you use your generosity

193
00:22:32,980 --> 00:22:38,980
as a power dynamic? It was something I had to really wrestle with of, do I use my generosity?

194
00:22:38,980 --> 00:22:45,980
Do I use my willingness and ability to help others as a power dynamic over those people? Or do I come at it like

195
00:22:45,980 --> 00:22:52,980
Jesus, have power under those people? And it's, I think it's something really interesting to wrestle with

196
00:22:52,980 --> 00:23:01,980
because I think receiving another person's story creates room. But I don't want you to hear is like,

197
00:23:01,980 --> 00:23:05,980
I don't think any of us have been, actually, I think we've been really good at the idea of,

198
00:23:05,980 --> 00:23:11,980
you've probably not heard a ton of people from this stage give you a five-point checklist, right? Am I right?

199
00:23:11,980 --> 00:23:17,980
Anybody hear a five-point checklist of any sermons recently? No. Well, I'm gonna answer for myself.

200
00:23:17,980 --> 00:23:23,980
That wasn't a rhetorical question, but I'm not getting any responses. So, I'm gonna go with no.

201
00:23:23,980 --> 00:23:30,980
The idea is, you can be imaginative. You don't have to follow Corey's

202
00:23:30,980 --> 00:23:35,980
playbook, or Ryan's playbook, or Kent's playbook. You can follow the Holy Spirit's playbook.

203
00:23:35,980 --> 00:23:40,980
And I'm convinced that the Holy Spirit does his most impactful work in areas where we don't have a

204
00:23:40,980 --> 00:23:50,980
playbook, but where we have to rely on him. I digress. Jesus enters Zacchaeus' house as a guest,

205
00:23:50,980 --> 00:23:59,980
but once he is there, Jesus becomes the true host. Zacchaeus opens his house to Jesus, but once he is

206
00:23:59,980 --> 00:24:08,980
there, Jesus opens the kingdom to Zacchaeus. The crowd calls him a sinful man, and Jesus calls him a son

207
00:24:08,980 --> 00:24:16,980
of Abraham. So I think Jesus is working to restore an identity that the crowd refused to recognize,

208
00:24:16,980 --> 00:24:23,980
that he is not whitewashing Zacchaeus' misdoing. He doesn't pretend that his exploitation

209
00:24:23,980 --> 00:24:30,980
doesn't matter. But Jesus sees a human being, an image bearer of God, where everyone else sees a

210
00:24:30,980 --> 00:24:39,980
category and an enemy. And we just read Romans 12 today. Everyone else sees a category. God in flesh

211
00:24:39,980 --> 00:24:46,980
sees an image bearer. Now, I know I've dropped this book recommendation before, but Rosario Butterfield

212
00:24:46,980 --> 00:24:53,980
describes biblical hospitality as seeing strangers become family and neighbors, as strangers

213
00:24:53,980 --> 00:24:59,980
becoming neighbors and neighbors becoming family. Her book is titled "The Gospel Comes with a House Key," and she

214
00:24:59,980 --> 00:25:07,980
calls this "Radically Ordinary Hospitality." And if you have not added that book to your reading

215
00:25:07,980 --> 00:25:12,980
list, I encourage you to do so, because it is impactful. And I will get into the implications

216
00:25:12,980 --> 00:25:19,980
of that impact when I talk about this in a minute. But it is radical because it crosses the

217
00:25:19,980 --> 00:25:24,980
boundaries in which we arrange our lives. It makes room for people who cannot repay us, people

218
00:25:24,980 --> 00:25:33,980
who disagree with us, people whose lives are complicated, and people whose complicated lives

219
00:25:33,980 --> 00:25:38,980
may interrupt our plans. But it's ordinary because it usually takes place through the

220
00:25:38,980 --> 00:25:44,980
things we're already doing. We already eat, we already drink coffee, we already sit on porches,

221
00:25:44,980 --> 00:25:49,980
attend ball games, walk through our neighborhoods, watch movies, work on projects, and take our kids

222
00:25:49,980 --> 00:25:55,980
to the playground. Hospitality doesn't always, if you're hearing it, I imagine that the crowd

223
00:25:55,980 --> 00:26:00,980
probably came with a sense of anxiety once I started talking about hospitality, "Oh no, Cory's

224
00:26:00,980 --> 00:26:04,980
going to give us another thing to do." No, hospitality does not always require adding

225
00:26:04,980 --> 00:26:09,980
another program to an already overcrowded schedule. It begins when we repurpose the

226
00:26:09,980 --> 00:26:15,980
ordinary rhythms of our lives and space as a welcome for somebody else. The question is

227
00:26:15,980 --> 00:26:21,980
not, "Can I produce an oppressive experience?" The question becomes, "Can I make room at this

228
00:26:21,980 --> 00:26:29,980
thing for them?" But I think we need to see what happens next in Zacchaeus' house. Zacchaeus says, "Look,

229
00:26:29,980 --> 00:26:34,980
I'll give half my possessions to the poor. And if I have extorted anything from anyone,

230
00:26:34,980 --> 00:26:42,980
I'll pay back four times as much." So I think this is where critics of proclaiming and embodying

231
00:26:42,980 --> 00:26:48,980
the gospel come off. The welcome of Jesus is not moral indifference. I don't have to fix

232
00:26:48,980 --> 00:26:56,980
everything about you the moment we meet. They're not projects. See, grace, the kind of grace

233
00:26:56,980 --> 00:27:02,980
that Jesus offers does not tell Zacchaeus that exploitation no longer matters because

234
00:27:02,980 --> 00:27:11,980
everybody makes mistakes. The grace tells Zacchaeus that his sin is not the truest thing about him.

235
00:27:11,980 --> 00:27:20,980
His sin is not the truest thing about him, and he does not have to remain the man he has become.

236
00:27:21,980 --> 00:27:27,980
Jesus moves towards Zacchaeus before the crowd sees any evidence that he deserves it.

237
00:27:27,980 --> 00:27:35,980
See, grace comes first. But I want to be very clear here.

238
00:27:35,980 --> 00:27:40,980
Grace also transforms Zacchaeus.

239
00:27:40,980 --> 00:27:47,980
See, I think we get into a mindset in our... I'm going to put this on me. I won't put this on you guys.

240
00:27:47,980 --> 00:27:53,980
We get into a mindset where our churchianity and Christian terms mean one thing.

241
00:27:53,980 --> 00:27:56,980
We say them and we mean something.

242
00:27:56,980 --> 00:28:05,980
And I'm becoming more convicted of this idea of every time I see grace in the biblical account, it is not...

243
00:28:05,980 --> 00:28:10,980
I receive this thing and I go on my life as if nothing happened.

244
00:28:10,980 --> 00:28:16,980
Biblical grace is not static. It is not lethargic. It does not just sit and collect.

245
00:28:17,980 --> 00:28:20,980
Biblical grace is always active.

246
00:28:20,980 --> 00:28:26,980
And I throw this term around when I'm wrestling with it because it's become the truest way I can define what I mean.

247
00:28:26,980 --> 00:28:29,980
It's the idea of reciprocal grace.

248
00:28:29,980 --> 00:28:34,980
Grace is extended by God, and I've now accepted it.

249
00:28:34,980 --> 00:28:40,980
And to show I've accepted it, I now acknowledge it and then extend it to others.

250
00:28:40,980 --> 00:28:43,980
Are we seeing the reciprocal idea of that?

251
00:28:44,980 --> 00:28:51,980
See, Zacchaeus is offered grace, and the way we know he's accepted the grace is because he begins to extend it.

252
00:28:51,980 --> 00:28:56,980
See, Zacchaeus' repentance becomes economic.

253
00:28:56,980 --> 00:29:03,980
The man who became rich by taking begins to give.

254
00:29:03,980 --> 00:29:08,980
The man who used power to exploit others begins seeking ways to make restitution.

255
00:29:09,980 --> 00:29:16,980
The evidence that salvation has entered the house is that damaged relationship and economic wrongdoings are being righted.

256
00:29:16,980 --> 00:29:22,980
Hospitality, therefore, does not require us to call everything good.

257
00:29:22,980 --> 00:29:28,980
Welcome does not mean the affirmation of every belief, desire, or decision.

258
00:29:28,980 --> 00:29:31,980
And love is not the avoidance of truth.

259
00:29:32,980 --> 00:29:43,980
And at the same time, again, the tension, at the same time, truth does not require us to hold, withhold relationship until that other person has made themselves right.

260
00:29:43,980 --> 00:29:51,980
Jesus does not shout instructions at Zacchaeus from the road or Facebook.

261
00:29:52,980 --> 00:30:05,980
Jesus enters his house. His gracious presence becomes the context in which repentance takes visible form.

262
00:30:05,980 --> 00:30:18,980
And that matters because if our hospitality is merely disguised as a technique for fixing people, people are going to eventually feel that manipulation.

263
00:30:19,980 --> 00:30:24,980
Contrary to what you may have been taught, people are not evangelistic projects.

264
00:30:24,980 --> 00:30:36,980
A meal should still be worth sharing even if the person never attends Canvas, or agrees with you, or follows Jesus.

265
00:30:36,980 --> 00:30:42,980
But refusing manipulation does not mean hiding the real Jesus.

266
00:30:42,980 --> 00:30:48,980
Real friendship includes honest disclosure about what matters most to us.

267
00:30:50,980 --> 00:30:57,980
And I think hospitality creates room for questions, prayer, testimony, disagreement, confession, and truth.

268
00:30:57,980 --> 00:31:02,980
And it also does not eliminate boundaries. Human beings have limits.

269
00:31:02,980 --> 00:31:10,980
Homes need safety. Trust can be damaged. Forgiveness and reconciliation are related, but they are not identical.

270
00:31:11,980 --> 00:31:19,980
And as I was writing this out, I think I put a guardrail in my message that I went back later and then disagreed with myself.

271
00:31:19,980 --> 00:31:26,980
Oh, I ran into my stand. I put in there that Christian hospitality is not reckless.

272
00:31:26,980 --> 00:31:34,980
And I sat with that and I'm like, is that me safeguarding where I think people are?

273
00:31:34,980 --> 00:31:39,980
Or is that maybe an honest description in the traditions of the church for 2000 years?

274
00:31:40,980 --> 00:31:44,980
And I think it was me safeguarding feelings, and I don't think it was true.

275
00:31:44,980 --> 00:31:49,980
So I took it out and I hesitate to say that Christian hospitality is not careless.

276
00:31:49,980 --> 00:32:02,980
Read enough Christian history and sometimes, I'm going to be honest, when I read Christian history and testimony of martyrs and forbearers of the faith, it looks remarkably careless.

277
00:32:03,980 --> 00:32:17,980
Christians who welcomed strangers during times of persecution. During the Black Plague, Christians are the ones who remained to take care of the sick while all else fled.

278
00:32:17,980 --> 00:32:28,980
Christians throughout 2000 years have shared food with people they couldn't spare extra food to feed them.

279
00:32:29,980 --> 00:32:36,980
Christians have risked their homes, reputations, freedom, and sometimes their lives to make room for another person.

280
00:32:36,980 --> 00:32:50,980
And this is where I want to guardrail this, and this is the conclusion I've come to. The resurrection of Jesus means self-preservation is no longer a highest good or our top priority.

281
00:32:50,980 --> 00:32:55,980
I want to say that again because I think this is something in the Western church we need to recognize.

282
00:32:56,980 --> 00:33:03,980
The resurrection of Jesus means that self-preservation is no longer our highest good or top priority.

283
00:33:03,980 --> 00:33:08,980
We can absolutely discuss what the implications of that after the service if you want to.

284
00:33:08,980 --> 00:33:13,980
But I want you to think about Revelation 4 and the slain lamb.

285
00:33:13,980 --> 00:33:24,980
If we believe death has been defeated, then we are free to risk things that fear tells us we have to protect at any cost.

286
00:33:25,980 --> 00:33:28,980
If we believe that, we will live like that.

287
00:33:28,980 --> 00:33:32,980
I heard this the other day, and I couldn't get it out of my mind.

288
00:33:32,980 --> 00:33:37,980
All the things you fear also fear Jesus.

289
00:33:37,980 --> 00:33:43,980
All the things you fear also fear Jesus.

290
00:33:43,980 --> 00:33:48,980
So Christian hospitality may be careless with our comfort.

291
00:33:48,980 --> 00:33:54,980
It may be careless with our schedules, our possessions, our carefully managed and crafted reputations,

292
00:33:54,980 --> 00:33:58,980
and our illusion that we can keep life under control and comfortable.

293
00:33:58,980 --> 00:34:02,980
But it should never be careless with another person's dignity.

294
00:34:02,980 --> 00:34:07,980
It should not ask vulnerable people to bear risk we are unwilling to bear ourselves.

295
00:34:07,980 --> 00:34:13,980
And it should not confuse courage with the need to prove we are courageous.

296
00:34:13,980 --> 00:34:21,980
Resurrection-shaped hospitality is neither safe nor thoughtless.

297
00:34:23,980 --> 00:34:30,980
It is a willingness to place what we have into the hands of Jesus, knowing that sometimes it will cost more than we expected to pay.

298
00:34:30,980 --> 00:34:36,980
Sometimes that love invites a person through the door.

299
00:34:36,980 --> 00:34:40,980
Sometimes that same love must tell the truth about what happened inside of it.

300
00:34:40,980 --> 00:34:46,980
I talked to you about Rosario Butterfield's "The Gospel Comes with a House Key."

301
00:34:46,980 --> 00:34:52,980
My first lady, my lovely wife, several years read this book.

302
00:34:52,980 --> 00:34:56,980
Several years ago, Samantha and I invited someone to live.

303
00:34:56,980 --> 00:35:00,980
I'm going to try not to cry through this, because I often thought that I cry up here too much.

304
00:35:00,980 --> 00:35:03,980
Samantha and I invited, I'm not going to look at any of you.

305
00:35:03,980 --> 00:35:08,980
Samantha and I, definitely not looking over here, Samantha and I invited someone to live with us.

306
00:35:08,980 --> 00:35:12,980
And when I say we knew her, I mean we were vaguely familiar with her.

307
00:35:12,980 --> 00:35:19,980
I also ran this part by her to make sure this got her stamp of approval, so don't think I'm weirdly gossiping.

308
00:35:21,980 --> 00:35:27,980
But she was part of our church. She was not yet a close friend. She was someone we interacted with.

309
00:35:27,980 --> 00:35:35,980
We knew her name, we knew a little of her story, but we certainly did not know her well enough to be inviting her into our home, making that feel natural.

310
00:35:35,980 --> 00:35:41,980
She had experienced a genuine encounter with Jesus during a sermon about the healing of a paralyzed man.

311
00:35:41,980 --> 00:35:46,980
She chose to be baptized. She wanted to follow Jesus.

312
00:35:47,980 --> 00:35:55,980
His desire began confronting the way she was living. In particular, she became convicted about who she was living with in her situation.

313
00:35:55,980 --> 00:35:59,980
Around that time, Samantha and I had been talking about opening our home to someone.

314
00:35:59,980 --> 00:36:04,980
It was one of those conversations where you sense Jesus might be asking you to do something through your wife,

315
00:36:04,980 --> 00:36:11,980
but you're still kind of hoping that he'll begin with something relatively more manageable than that.

316
00:36:12,980 --> 00:36:18,980
But it seemed short-term, seemed like a chance for us to dip our toes in the water, if you will.

317
00:36:18,980 --> 00:36:24,980
So we invited her into our home. And at first, we probably imagined we were giving her a place to stay. I know I did.

318
00:36:24,980 --> 00:36:30,980
And again, think of that power dynamic. But she did not remain a stranger for long.

319
00:36:30,980 --> 00:36:35,980
She eventually became, we made the joke, she became our roommate.

320
00:36:35,980 --> 00:36:39,980
She eventually became our friend, and eventually she became family.

321
00:36:40,980 --> 00:36:47,980
We ate dinners together, we watched movies, we talked about life, we shared ordinary rhythms that will never make it into dramatic testimonies.

322
00:36:47,980 --> 00:36:51,980
But they are something that bind people together forever.

323
00:36:51,980 --> 00:36:59,980
Discipleship began happening around a dinner table, and on the couch, it happened in conversations that weren't on anybody's church calendar.

324
00:36:59,980 --> 00:37:06,980
We had opened our home because we believed she needed something from us. But Jesus knew that we needed something.

325
00:37:07,980 --> 00:37:11,980
I get it. But Jesus knew that we needed something from her, too.

326
00:37:11,980 --> 00:37:16,980
It was not always easy, and eventually, we reached a painful point in our relationship.

327
00:37:16,980 --> 00:37:22,980
Choices had been made, trust had been wounded. Things could simply not continue as though nothing had happened.

328
00:37:22,980 --> 00:37:27,980
See, I think Christian hospitality does not mean pretending everything is fine.

329
00:37:27,980 --> 00:37:33,980
Welcome is not the absence of truth, boundaries, or consequences.

330
00:37:34,980 --> 00:37:41,980
I think Jesus welcomed Zacchaeus before Zacchaeus had made everything right, but the presence of Jesus did not leave Zacchaeus unchanged.

331
00:37:41,980 --> 00:37:50,980
Grace moved towards him, and that grace eventually took the form of confession, repentance, restitution, and a different life.

332
00:37:50,980 --> 00:37:55,980
For our friend, healing eventually meant returning home to her family several states away.

333
00:37:55,980 --> 00:38:02,980
She would and is always welcome with us, but sometimes, healing requires a new location.

334
00:38:03,980 --> 00:38:06,980
Different voices and enough distance so we can see ourselves truthfully.

335
00:38:06,980 --> 00:38:10,980
Sometimes, love welcomes someone through the front door.

336
00:38:10,980 --> 00:38:18,980
Sometimes, that same love blesses them as they walk through it towards the place where Jesus is still continuing his work.

337
00:38:18,980 --> 00:38:27,980
And she did the hard work, not the glamorous work, not the work that produces an immediate, polished testimony.

338
00:38:28,980 --> 00:38:41,980
But I will say I was privileged to watch her do the slow work of confession, repentance, self-reflection, and allowing Jesus into the places she would probably have preferred he stay out of.

339
00:38:41,980 --> 00:38:44,980
And Jesus met her there.

340
00:38:44,980 --> 00:38:50,980
In that work, she discovered the depth of God's redemption.

341
00:38:50,980 --> 00:38:53,980
She experienced the love and the favor of God.

342
00:38:54,980 --> 00:38:56,980
And somewhere along that journey, she met her husband.

343
00:38:56,980 --> 00:38:58,980
She met the man who would become her husband.

344
00:38:58,980 --> 00:39:00,980
She didn't meet her husband. That would be weird.

345
00:39:00,980 --> 00:39:01,980
That sounds like, never mind. I'm not going to make that joke.

346
00:39:01,980 --> 00:39:05,980
Last year, Samantha and I were invited to their wedding.

347
00:39:05,980 --> 00:39:11,980
And I, you know me, I was not emotionally prepared for it.

348
00:39:11,980 --> 00:39:16,980
As I watched her get married, all these memories ran through my mind.

349
00:39:20,980 --> 00:39:27,980
The person who had once been practically a stranger to us, we gave her a key to our house.

350
00:39:27,980 --> 00:39:29,980
We shared meals.

351
00:39:29,980 --> 00:39:32,980
We watched movies. We just did ordinary life.

352
00:39:32,980 --> 00:39:34,980
We watched her make mistakes.

353
00:39:34,980 --> 00:39:36,980
We experienced hurt.

354
00:39:36,980 --> 00:39:43,980
We watched her leave, and then we watched from a distance as Jesus patiently brought healing and redemption out of what had been broken.

355
00:39:43,980 --> 00:39:47,980
Now this stranger had become family.

356
00:39:48,980 --> 00:39:52,980
I did not tell you this story because I want you to think Samantha and I rescued anyone.

357
00:39:52,980 --> 00:39:54,980
We certainly did not.

358
00:39:54,980 --> 00:39:56,980
Jesus did.

359
00:39:56,980 --> 00:40:06,980
We did not engineer the redemption we witnessed at that wedding or the redemption that is still unfolding in many ways that you and I are not fully privy to.

360
00:40:06,980 --> 00:40:14,980
Much of it happened several states away through other people, her family, her future husband, and above all through her own difficult work with Jesus.

361
00:40:14,980 --> 00:40:16,980
We just simply opened a door.

362
00:40:17,980 --> 00:40:29,980
But because we opened the door, I was given, we were given a front row seat to the grace of God that I cannot fully explain to you with words.

363
00:40:29,980 --> 00:40:34,980
We experienced the kind of discipleship that could never happen in a Bible study.

364
00:40:34,980 --> 00:40:46,980
We had to learn on the fly how to welcome, how to trust, how to tell the truth, how to establish boundaries, how to forgive and how to release someone.

365
00:40:46,980 --> 00:40:49,980
We loved deeply into the continuing work of Jesus.

366
00:40:49,980 --> 00:40:52,980
We thought we were making room for her.

367
00:40:52,980 --> 00:40:56,980
But Jesus was using her to make more room in us as well.

368
00:40:56,980 --> 00:41:02,980
See, hospitality, as I said earlier, did not save her. Jesus did.

369
00:41:02,980 --> 00:41:09,980
But hospitality gave the work of Jesus room to take flesh in our ordinary lives.

370
00:41:09,980 --> 00:41:14,980
The gospel comes with a house key because the gospel is not merely an idea we believe.

371
00:41:15,980 --> 00:41:27,980
It's an idea of the life we share. It takes the shape of meals, conversations, spare bedrooms, wounded trust, difficult truth, tearful goodbyes, long distance prayers, and hopefully and God willingly wedding invitations.

372
00:41:27,980 --> 00:41:32,980
Sometimes salvation comes to a house and no one living in that house remains the same.

373
00:41:32,980 --> 00:41:39,980
Folks, this is why the church cannot exist for the sake of maintaining the institution.

374
00:41:40,980 --> 00:41:46,980
And that's, if you know me, that's difficult for me to say because I love the church.

375
00:41:46,980 --> 00:41:53,980
Jesus did not call isolated individuals to share their private beliefs.

376
00:41:53,980 --> 00:41:58,980
He formed a people, a covenant people.

377
00:41:58,980 --> 00:42:05,980
We need gathered worship, Scripture, communion, correction, encouragement, and life together.

378
00:42:06,980 --> 00:42:09,980
We need the body of Christ.

379
00:42:09,980 --> 00:42:15,980
The church is not a religious club organized around the comfort of its existing members.

380
00:42:15,980 --> 00:42:17,980
And we do a pretty good job of that.

381
00:42:17,980 --> 00:42:26,980
God gathers and forms us so that we can participate in his mission of reconciling and healing the world.

382
00:42:26,980 --> 00:42:32,980
If you can get a hold of that idea, it is awesome and terrifying.

383
00:42:34,980 --> 00:42:38,980
We are not fully healed, and we are certainly not the healers.

384
00:42:38,980 --> 00:42:40,980
Jesus is.

385
00:42:40,980 --> 00:42:44,980
But we are a community in which the healing has begun.

386
00:42:44,980 --> 00:42:51,980
Through which healing can become visible to a world that desperately needs to be healed.

387
00:42:51,980 --> 00:42:58,980
That means the life of the church cannot remain comfortably inside these walls for just a Sunday gathering.

388
00:42:58,980 --> 00:43:03,980
Our worship must eventually become embodied obedience.

389
00:43:04,980 --> 00:43:08,980
Our prayers for the lonely must eventually produce an open chair.

390
00:43:08,980 --> 00:43:12,980
Our concern for the poor must eventually touch our budgets.

391
00:43:12,980 --> 00:43:17,980
Our belief in reconciliation must eventually cross a social boundary we don't feel good about crossing.

392
00:43:17,980 --> 00:43:25,980
Our claim that everyone bears the image of God must eventually change the way we receive the person others have reduced to a label.

393
00:43:25,980 --> 00:43:32,980
Romans 12:13 does not merely tell Christians to permit hospitality.

394
00:43:33,980 --> 00:43:37,980
Paul says to pursue it, to seek it, to make a deliberate effort towards it.

395
00:43:37,980 --> 00:43:41,980
And church, I would ask you the same question I have asked myself.

396
00:43:41,980 --> 00:43:46,980
Am I, are we making an effort towards it?

397
00:43:46,980 --> 00:43:55,980
And this is not a heart of shame or guilt, but like all good news and invitation into the abundant life that Jesus has promised.

398
00:43:55,980 --> 00:44:00,980
I was privileged to experience God's redemption on a way I could not explain to you.

399
00:44:01,980 --> 00:44:04,980
I could not have told me I had to experience it.

400
00:44:04,980 --> 00:44:11,980
The New Testament word often translated hospitality carries the sense of love for the stranger.

401
00:44:11,980 --> 00:44:22,980
It stands in opposition to our fear of the stranger, which the flailing dragon would love to tell you to fear the stranger.

402
00:44:22,980 --> 00:44:24,980
Jesus tells you to love the stranger.

403
00:44:24,980 --> 00:44:30,980
Hospitality is not merely loving people who already belong comfortably inside of our circle.

404
00:44:31,980 --> 00:44:36,980
It is an intentional movement towards the person who is still well outside that circle.

405
00:44:36,980 --> 00:44:41,980
It does not mean that every Christian must invite a stranger into their home this week.

406
00:44:41,980 --> 00:44:43,980
Ryan kind of gave you the guardrails last week.

407
00:44:43,980 --> 00:44:47,980
Don't just be like, "I'm going to go do this thing." Pray about it.

408
00:44:47,980 --> 00:44:50,980
God, what have you called me to?

409
00:44:50,980 --> 00:44:56,980
Please do not leave believing that the only faithful response is to copy somebody else's story.

410
00:44:57,980 --> 00:45:01,980
Hospitality must be practiced with wisdom and agreement in the household,

411
00:45:01,980 --> 00:45:03,980
appropriate boundaries, and attention to safety,

412
00:45:03,980 --> 00:45:06,980
but most of all in alignment with the word the Spirit has given you.

413
00:45:06,980 --> 00:45:11,980
There were circumstances in our story that made the invitation possible.

414
00:45:11,980 --> 00:45:15,980
And there are seasons where that invitation would not be wise.

415
00:45:15,980 --> 00:45:19,980
But the command to practice hospitality belongs to all of us.

416
00:45:19,980 --> 00:45:22,980
The form will differ, the posture should not.

417
00:45:22,980 --> 00:45:26,980
The form will differ, but the posture should not.

418
00:45:26,980 --> 00:45:28,980
We are still called to hospitality.

419
00:45:28,980 --> 00:45:30,980
For one person, hospitality may be a spare room.

420
00:45:30,980 --> 00:45:33,980
For another, it may be an extra plate at dinner.

421
00:45:33,980 --> 00:45:38,980
For others, it may be a walk after work, a seat beside you at your child's baseball game,

422
00:45:38,980 --> 00:45:43,980
or football game, or letting someone help you with a project, or helping them.

423
00:45:43,980 --> 00:45:49,980
It may mean noticing the person standing alone in church lobby rather than just talking to your closest friends.

424
00:45:49,980 --> 00:45:55,980
It may be receiving an invitation into someone else's home

425
00:45:55,980 --> 00:45:57,980
and allowing them to serve you.

426
00:45:57,980 --> 00:46:03,980
It may simply mean becoming the kind of person around whom someone can tell you the truth

427
00:46:03,980 --> 00:46:06,980
without immediately being reduced to the thing they did wrong.

428
00:46:06,980 --> 00:46:13,980
Hospitality is a posture well in advance of being a performance.

429
00:46:13,980 --> 00:46:20,980
But our culture, consumed with its consumerism and materialism, has scared you out of the biblical mandate.

430
00:46:23,980 --> 00:46:27,980
The question is not whether or not your house looks ready.

431
00:46:27,980 --> 00:46:32,980
That's what Instagram and Better Homes and Gardens magazine would tell you.

432
00:46:32,980 --> 00:46:40,980
I would say the biblical question is whether your life has any room in it for someone else.

433
00:46:40,980 --> 00:46:47,980
Because I believe this down to my bones that Christ is inconvenient.

434
00:46:47,980 --> 00:46:50,980
I want you to sit with that a second.

435
00:46:51,980 --> 00:46:53,980
I think Christ is inconvenient.

436
00:46:53,980 --> 00:46:58,980
I think we should be honest with ourselves. Making room for someone else is inconvenient.

437
00:46:58,980 --> 00:47:03,980
People arrive when the house is messy. Conversations take longer than expected.

438
00:47:03,980 --> 00:47:06,980
Kids still need to go to bed. Lunches still need to be packed. Dishes still need to be washed.

439
00:47:06,980 --> 00:47:12,980
And other people's needs don't necessarily revolve around your calendar.

440
00:47:12,980 --> 00:47:15,980
I don't know if you knew that, but they don't.

441
00:47:15,980 --> 00:47:20,980
When we welcome a real person rather than an imaginary guest we've made up in our minds,

442
00:47:20,980 --> 00:47:22,980
that we welcome a life we cannot control anymore.

443
00:47:22,980 --> 00:47:27,980
But I would say Christ is inconvenient too.

444
00:47:27,980 --> 00:47:32,980
So I think Jesus continually interrupts the boundaries people use to keep life manageable.

445
00:47:32,980 --> 00:47:36,980
He stops for the blind man others tell to be quiet.

446
00:47:36,980 --> 00:47:40,980
He allows an unclean woman to touch him in a crowd.

447
00:47:40,980 --> 00:47:46,980
He speaks with a Samaritan woman when other respectable people would have avoided her.

448
00:47:49,980 --> 00:47:57,980
He tells stories in which the neighbor is the person that their religious and ethnic hostility has taught us to distrust.

449
00:47:57,980 --> 00:48:05,980
He looks up into a tree and sees a corrupt tax collector and tells him, "I must stay at your house today."

450
00:48:05,980 --> 00:48:12,980
Samuel Jackson Reed names this beautifully in a poem called "Christ is the Inconvenient Yeast."

451
00:48:12,980 --> 00:48:18,980
He writes, "Christ is the Inconvenient Yeast asking for more than we first see."

452
00:48:19,980 --> 00:48:24,980
Later, he calls us to give what is received like loaves and fishes.

453
00:48:24,980 --> 00:48:29,980
The yeast disappears into the dough, but it changes everything it touches.

454
00:48:29,980 --> 00:48:39,980
The life of Jesus works its way into our schedules, our homes, our relationships, and our assumption about who belongs.

455
00:48:39,980 --> 00:48:47,980
His kingdom will not remain a private belief system tucked safely inside of your neatly organized file cap.

456
00:48:48,980 --> 00:48:55,980
But it expands, it presses outward, and it makes room for people who do not belong.

457
00:48:55,980 --> 00:49:01,980
Thank God, because I do not belong. You do not belong.

458
00:49:01,980 --> 00:49:15,980
The poem invites us to recognize Christ in the face of the leper, the laborer, the person buried beneath the bombs of empire, the widow, the orphan, the destitute, and the despised.

459
00:49:16,980 --> 00:49:27,980
Not because every person becomes Jesus some way, but it is because Jesus has chosen to identify himself with the person that the world overlooks.

460
00:49:27,980 --> 00:49:32,980
He tells us what we do for the least of these we do for him.

461
00:49:32,980 --> 00:49:38,980
The stranger is not merely an interruption preventing us from doing ministry.

462
00:49:38,980 --> 00:49:42,980
Receiving the stranger may be the place we actually receive Christ.

463
00:49:45,980 --> 00:49:53,980
Hebrews 13 tells us, "Do not neglect hospitality to strangers because some have welcomed angels without knowing it."

464
00:49:53,980 --> 00:50:01,980
See, Scripture continually trains us to approach the stranger with a holy attentiveness.

465
00:50:01,980 --> 00:50:08,980
We do not know beforehand how God may meet us in the person we welcome.

466
00:50:08,980 --> 00:50:11,980
This is what happened to us.

467
00:50:12,980 --> 00:50:18,980
We thought we were offering a room, but instead Jesus was offering us deep transformation.

468
00:50:18,980 --> 00:50:25,980
We thought we were giving someone else a place in our household, but Jesus was expanding our understanding of family.

469
00:50:25,980 --> 00:50:29,980
We thought, "We can dip our toes in the water."

470
00:50:29,980 --> 00:50:33,980
But I like to say this, Jesus was teaching us how to swim really fast.

471
00:50:33,980 --> 00:50:40,980
See, the inconvenience was not a distraction from discipleship for me, for Jesus, for you.

472
00:50:41,980 --> 00:50:42,980
It was discipleship.

473
00:50:42,980 --> 00:50:51,980
So how do we respond without turning this message into another, adding another burden to an already overburdened schedule?

474
00:50:51,980 --> 00:50:55,980
I would encourage you to begin small.

475
00:50:55,980 --> 00:50:58,980
Do not plan an impressive ministry event.

476
00:50:58,980 --> 00:51:00,980
It's not what anybody's asking you to do.

477
00:51:00,980 --> 00:51:05,980
I want you to think of one person, and I think Pastor Ryan gave this last week.

478
00:51:05,980 --> 00:51:07,980
I'm going to narrow it down.

479
00:51:07,980 --> 00:51:09,980
I think you gave three to five.

480
00:51:09,980 --> 00:51:10,980
I'm going to go one.

481
00:51:10,980 --> 00:51:12,980
I'm going to go one to make it really easy.

482
00:51:12,980 --> 00:51:16,980
I want you to think of one person God has already placed near you.

483
00:51:16,980 --> 00:51:19,980
Perhaps it's someone from the prayer list you began last week.

484
00:51:19,980 --> 00:51:23,980
Maybe it's someone you saw on Facebook that's struggling.

485
00:51:23,980 --> 00:51:31,980
Maybe it's somebody that is in a place that is awkwardly different, and you know it, and why not reach out to them?

486
00:51:31,980 --> 00:51:36,980
Perhaps it's a neighbor whose name you know, but whose story you do not.

487
00:51:37,980 --> 00:51:46,980
Perhaps it's a coworker who always eats alone, a parent who sees you at practices, someone who is new to our church, or a friend who has quietly drifted to the edge of community.

488
00:51:46,980 --> 00:51:50,980
And identify one rhythm already present in your week.

489
00:51:50,980 --> 00:51:53,980
You already eat dinner, I assume.

490
00:51:53,980 --> 00:51:57,980
You already drink coffee or energy drinks.

491
00:51:57,980 --> 00:51:59,980
You already take a lunch break.

492
00:51:59,980 --> 00:52:03,980
You already walk the dog, work in the garage, go play at the playground.

493
00:52:03,980 --> 00:52:06,980
Invite someone into what you were already doing.

494
00:52:08,980 --> 00:52:14,980
And when you are together, put the phone away, practice listening hospitality.

495
00:52:14,980 --> 00:52:16,980
Ask the real question.

496
00:52:16,980 --> 00:52:22,980
Don't spend the whole conversation rehearsing what you intend to say next, that's me, because I do that.

497
00:52:22,980 --> 00:52:25,980
Or what I should have said later.

498
00:52:25,980 --> 00:52:30,980
But, become curious about their story, who they are, what makes them them.

499
00:52:31,980 --> 00:52:39,980
I'm not going to embarrass her, but Samantha said that yesterday at the boys' soccer game, that people wouldn't act the way they do if they knew their stories.

500
00:52:39,980 --> 00:52:41,980
I picked on her most of the day about it.

501
00:52:41,980 --> 00:52:43,980
But, she's right.

502
00:52:43,980 --> 00:52:47,980
We wouldn't act the way we do if we knew people's stories.

503
00:52:47,980 --> 00:52:50,980
Where are they finding joy?

504
00:52:50,980 --> 00:52:52,980
Where does their life hurt?

505
00:52:52,980 --> 00:52:54,980
What do they fear?

506
00:52:54,980 --> 00:52:56,980
What do they hope for?

507
00:52:56,980 --> 00:52:59,980
And what has their experience with Christianity been?

508
00:53:00,980 --> 00:53:03,980
If faith comes up, I don't want you to be evasive.

509
00:53:03,980 --> 00:53:07,980
I don't think that Jesus should be an embarrassing secret to our lives.

510
00:53:07,980 --> 00:53:11,980
But I also don't think he's performative either.

511
00:53:11,980 --> 00:53:14,980
I want you to tell the truth naturally.

512
00:53:14,980 --> 00:53:16,980
Share what Jesus has done in your life.

513
00:53:16,980 --> 00:53:18,980
Offer to pray when it is appropriate.

514
00:53:18,980 --> 00:53:25,980
Trust that the Holy Spirit was and is present before you arrive and remains with them well after the conversation ends.

515
00:53:25,980 --> 00:53:27,980
And then keep making room.

516
00:53:29,980 --> 00:53:35,980
See, I think hospitality is rarely one spectacular event that you're like, hospitality.

517
00:53:35,980 --> 00:53:38,980
No, it's a repeated presence.

518
00:53:38,980 --> 00:53:41,980
Where strangers become neighbors slowly.

519
00:53:41,980 --> 00:53:43,980
Neighbors become family through shared meals.

520
00:53:43,980 --> 00:53:44,980
Remember details.

521
00:53:44,980 --> 00:53:45,980
Ordinary laughter.

522
00:53:45,980 --> 00:53:46,980
Inconvenient phone calls.

523
00:53:46,980 --> 00:53:52,980
And the confidence that disagreement will not make this person disappear, but they know I love them and they love me.

524
00:53:52,980 --> 00:53:56,980
Your table does not have to be impressive.

525
00:53:56,980 --> 00:53:58,980
Your house does not have to be spotless.

526
00:53:58,980 --> 00:54:03,980
Your family does not have to perform, although it would be easier if they just listened.

527
00:54:03,980 --> 00:54:15,980
In fact, allowing someone to see the ordinary mess of real Christian household may be far more hospitable than creating the experience they could have never imagined joining.

528
00:54:15,980 --> 00:54:26,980
There are people, there are people in this room that would see the nice house and the table and go, "God, I could never be like that."

529
00:54:27,980 --> 00:54:30,980
A message and a lifestyle that I could never join.

530
00:54:30,980 --> 00:54:37,980
We're not inviting people to admire our lives.

531
00:54:37,980 --> 00:54:41,980
We're inviting them to encounter the life of Jesus among us.

532
00:54:41,980 --> 00:54:46,980
So, here's my conclusion.

533
00:54:46,980 --> 00:54:53,980
When Jesus entered Zacchaeus' home, the crowd saw contamination, but Jesus saw salvation.

534
00:54:54,980 --> 00:55:00,980
They saw a boundary that should not be crossed, and Jesus saw a son of Abraham who desperately needed to be restored.

535
00:55:00,980 --> 00:55:05,980
They believe righteousness meant separating from Zacchaeus.

536
00:55:05,980 --> 00:55:12,980
But instead, Jesus demonstrates holiness strong enough to enter a sinner's house and transform it.

537
00:55:12,980 --> 00:55:16,980
And when salvation came to that house, it became visible.

538
00:55:16,980 --> 00:55:20,980
A man who had taken begins to give.

539
00:55:21,980 --> 00:55:24,980
A man who had exploited his neighbors begins to restore.

540
00:55:24,980 --> 00:55:31,980
A man known only as a sinner is now reclaimed as a son.

541
00:55:31,980 --> 00:55:38,980
The reign of Jesus was demonstrated in that house, and the king was a guest at his table.

542
00:55:38,980 --> 00:55:42,980
This is the hope of Christian hospitality.

543
00:55:42,980 --> 00:55:48,980
We do not open our lives because we are people, project builders.

544
00:55:49,980 --> 00:55:52,980
We open our lives because Jesus has opened his kingdom to us.

545
00:55:52,980 --> 00:55:55,980
We were the strangers brought near.

546
00:55:55,980 --> 00:56:00,980
We were the sinners welcomed before any one of us could make ourselves worthy.

547
00:56:00,980 --> 00:56:06,980
We were the people with nothing adequate to place on the table, yet Jesus made room for us.

548
00:56:06,980 --> 00:56:12,980
I think about this when we sing the song, "What else do I have for a king but a hallelujah?"

549
00:56:12,980 --> 00:56:14,980
Got nothing else.

550
00:56:16,980 --> 00:56:20,980
Now Jesus teaches us to become co-hosts of his welcome.

551
00:56:20,980 --> 00:56:28,980
We offer what we have: a table, an extra chair, a pot of food, an afternoon, a listening ear, an ordinary life.

552
00:56:28,980 --> 00:56:34,980
It may not feel like enough, but five loaves and two fishes didn't look like enough either.

553
00:56:34,980 --> 00:56:39,980
The question is not whether we possess enough to heal the world. We do not.

554
00:56:39,980 --> 00:56:44,980
The question is whether we will place what we have into the hands of Jesus and allow him to give it away.

555
00:56:45,980 --> 00:56:48,980
That's hard. That's hard.

556
00:56:48,980 --> 00:56:54,980
Will we take what we have and get it into the hands of Jesus so that he can give it away?

557
00:56:54,980 --> 00:57:01,980
Because I want you to think of who might be waiting in a tree, curious about Jesus, but unable to see past the crowd.

558
00:57:01,980 --> 00:57:05,980
Who might need to hear their names spoken with love.

559
00:57:05,980 --> 00:57:11,980
Who might need something more tangible than an argument before they can believe the welcome of God is real.

560
00:57:12,980 --> 00:57:17,980
What ordinary part of your life could be the place where that welcomed is experienced.

561
00:57:17,980 --> 00:57:25,980
This week, I challenge you, find one person, choose one ordinary rhythm, make room without manipulation.

562
00:57:25,980 --> 00:57:30,980
Tell the truth, welcome wisdom, trust, trust the Holy Spirit.

563
00:57:30,980 --> 00:57:34,980
You may discover that Jesus is already at work in the person you welcomed.

564
00:57:34,980 --> 00:57:39,980
You may discover that while you were making room for them, Jesus is also making room in you.

565
00:57:40,980 --> 00:57:46,980
Because sometimes salvation comes to a house, and nobody living in that house remains the same.

566
00:57:46,980 --> 00:57:48,980
Let's pray.

567
00:57:48,980 --> 00:57:53,980
Thank you, Jesus.

568
00:57:53,980 --> 00:57:57,980
Thank you for welcoming us when we were strangers.

569
00:57:57,980 --> 00:58:01,980
You've called us by name when others could only see our failures.

570
00:58:01,980 --> 00:58:05,980
You brought us close to your table and made us members of your family.

571
00:58:07,980 --> 00:58:12,980
Forgive us for the ways we have turned your gifts and your blessings into walls.

572
00:58:12,980 --> 00:58:17,980
Forgive us when our homes, our schedules, friendships, and church

573
00:58:17,980 --> 00:58:21,980
have been arranged only around our own comfort.

574
00:58:21,980 --> 00:58:27,980
Lord, give us eyes to see people as you see them, people who are already near.

575
00:58:27,980 --> 00:58:32,980
Teach us to make room. Give us wisdom and courage.

576
00:58:33,980 --> 00:58:37,980
Make us truthful without becoming harsh or cynical.

577
00:58:37,980 --> 00:58:40,980
Make us open without becoming careless.

578
00:58:40,980 --> 00:58:44,980
Make us hospitable without people becoming projects.

579
00:58:44,980 --> 00:58:50,980
Show each of us the one person and one ordinary thing we could do

580
00:58:50,980 --> 00:58:53,980
in which your welcome can be visible this week.

581
00:58:53,980 --> 00:58:56,980
As we open our lives to others, continue.

582
00:58:56,980 --> 00:59:00,980
Lord, we ask you to continue opening our lives to you.

583
00:59:02,980 --> 00:59:06,980
Your reign be demonstrated through our lives and declared through our words

584
00:59:06,980 --> 00:59:09,980
wherever we live, work, and play.

585
00:59:09,980 --> 00:59:15,980
I pray all of us for your glory, our neighbor's good, and our good.

586
00:59:15,980 --> 00:59:18,980
In Jesus' mighty name we pray. Amen.

587
00:59:18,980 --> 00:59:24,980
Unless the Reverend Doctor has anything. Church, go in peace.

588
00:59:24,980 --> 00:59:31,980
And in this week, remember, may we live out the reign of Jesus demonstrated through our lives

589
00:59:31,980 --> 00:59:33,980
and declared through our words.

590
00:59:33,980 --> 00:59:35,980
Thank you, church. Love you all.
